  "observed_mismatch_and_candidate_root_cause": {
    "bounded_sample_finding": "In the first 8 fit real rows, positive-centered 512 tiles had median 4,810 known pixels and median positive fraction 1.0; negative-centered tiles had median 29,600 known pixels and positive fraction 0.0 (6/8 had negatives). In the first 8 generated-CAD rows, the corresponding medians were 200,201.5 known / 0.465 positive fraction and 203,265 known / 0.113 positive fraction. Thus center-branch selection creates strongly different per-update class mixtures, especially for real tiles. Each tile BCE is normalized independently by its own known area; positive-bearing tiles also get a full-strength Dice term.",
    "candidate": "The most actionable precision failure candidate is query/branch/loss coupling: one query-conditioned training update sees only one positive- or negative-centered crop; 27 fit real queries have positive but no reviewed-negative domain and therefore only supply positive supervision; and every positive tile with any positive receives an unscaled Dice add-on while negative-only tiles receive BCE only. This can bias updates toward recall and does not teach each query to reject its hardest known lookalikes. The evidence establishes mechanics and class mixtures, not that this is the cause of any validation metric.",
    "other plausible factor": "For 512-trained matched continuations, 512-to-896 inference scale mismatch is independently actionable; it is absent for checkpoint 200 and the later 896 coverage checkpoint.",
    "source_proven": "The 200-step checkpoint was trained at 896 and is inference-scale matched. The later matched head/all 800-step continuations use 512 tiles while the native adapter runs 896, so scale mismatch applies to those continuations only.",
